\cf0 \strokec2 I'm ready to look into enhancements and improvements now. One of my favorite tools is JobRight. Their system is one of the more intelligent I've come across in terms of resume scoring based on specific positions, custom resume generation based on specific positions, job matching (the roles with high scores there ARE actually good fits, there's very little junk), resume evaluation/diagnosis/re-writing (just overall, before you even use it for a specific position). One difference I notice is that my resume-builder generates a fresh, custom resume for every role from scratch. JobRight, on the other hand, works with up to 5 resumes of various types -- and when you find a role that you want a custom resume for, you can see the score for each resume for that custom role, pick the best scoring resume to work with, and then customize it for the JD after a gap analysis. It's an interesting comparison! I'm curious how I can use the attached research to strengthen the logic behind what I've built so far? How can I take it further and be able to generate a resume from my program that I can trust, and follows the rules I'm trying to put into place? Customizing resumes takes me far too long because I try to put SO much thought into it. Automating this would change everything.\
4/4\
